\section*{Overview}
This README provides structural, organizational, and compilation guidance for the
\textit{Aether-Tetra Codex}, a multi-chapter LaTeX document describing the
topological, dynamical, and gauge-structured physics architecture of the
Aether-Tetra simulation engine. The Codex is written entirely in LaTeX and is
intended for PDF compilation using standard TeX distributions.

\section*{Document Structure}
The Codex is organized into sequential chapters, each addressing a distinct
architectural layer of the Aether-Tetra manifold:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Chapter VII}: Topological architectures and subsystem design.
    \item \textbf{Chapter VIII}: Dynamical regimes and propagation mechanics.
    \item \textbf{Chapter IX}: Stability basins, attractors, and fixed-point evolution.
    \item \textbf{Chapter X}: Global attractors and long-horizon evolution.
    \item \textbf{Chapter XI}: Constraint surfaces, global invariants, and conservation laws.
    \item \textbf{Chapter XII}: Gauge invariance, symmetry groups, and conserved currents.
\end{itemize}

Each chapter is self-contained and may be referenced independently, but the
Codex is designed to be read in sequence to reveal the full hierarchical
structure of the Aether-Tetra engine.

\section*{Compilation Instructions}
The Codex is compatible with all major LaTeX engines, including:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{pdflatex}
    \item \texttt{xelatex}
    \item \texttt{lualatex}
\end{itemize}

To compile the document into a PDF, run:

\begin{verbatim}
pdflatex codex.tex
\end{verbatim}

Repeat compilation if cross-references or table of contents entries are used.

\section*{Package Requirements}
The Codex uses only standard LaTeX packages:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \texttt{amsmath}, \texttt{amssymb}, \texttt{amsthm}
    \item \texttt{geometry}
    \item \texttt{hyperref}
    \item \texttt{setspace}
\end{itemize}

No external or non-standard packages are required.

\section*{Design Philosophy}
The Aether-Tetra Codex is structured according to the following principles:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Manifold-Native Representation}: All physics is expressed directly
    on tetrahedral simplicial complexes.
    \item \textbf{Topological Integrity}: Euler characteristic, homology, and
    manifold closure are preserved throughout.
    \item \textbf{Gauge Consistency}: Local diffeomorphism invariance and Lie-group
    symmetry actions are enforced across moving simplices.
    \item \textbf{Global Invariants}: Chirality sums, Hamiltonian constraints, and
    conserved currents remain stable across long-horizon evolution.
\end{itemize}

